**Definition 2.3 (relative commands and total status accounting).** A
public relative command $(a(t),b(t))$, starting at zero, means
$k(t)=x+a(t)$ and $\chi(t)=\chi_*+b(t)$. Its mathematical domain is
$(\ell_b-\min a,u_b-\max a)$, as in [Probability/Phase Transport][PP]
§9.1. A nonempty domain is a partial-action fact, not an unknown-height
detector. All lower tests here have common present permissions and are
promised legal at every tested height.

The canonical mathematical over-menu consists of permitted prescribed
original controls at the same base and matching current height, and finite
chronological compositions of them. Each separately supplied waveform and
processor keeps its actual permission, calibration and recording contract.
Finite feedback may choose a next supplied member using only actual
records and the declared controller/external inputs. Describing this
over-menu does not supply a missing source-bound execution service.

A positive horizontal request of displacement $d>0$ and duration $L>0$
has $(a,b)=(dt/L,0)$ and domain $(0,c-d)$. A phase request has
$(a,b)=(0,\alpha t/L)$, $L>0$, and domain $(0,c)$ for every finite
real $\alpha$. Neither requires the controller to know $x,\beta,m$
or the old real lift. At a matching join the real tail is
$\chi(T+u)=\chi(T)+\chi_{\rm tail}(u)-\chi_{\rm tail}(0)$.
Zero duration allows the matching identity, not an instantaneous nonzero leg.

Known permission denial or known interface/domain mismatch has its declared
visible unexecuted rejection, with the unchanged specimen and actual
controller update. A mathematically undefined request has no executed
successor asserted here. No detector for an unflagged promise violation is
constructed. On first executed failure retain its exact time, original mode,
old joint archive and absorbing specimen $f$. Only the declared stopped
continuation remains; no reset, reversal, post-failure retry, replacement
specimen or hidden environment/Kraus-index read is supplied. Feedback uses
only actually retained records and common declared external inputs.

